<BaseLayout
  title="OEM & Private Label Incense — LanDeng"
  description="OEM and private-label manufacturing for Chinese botanical incense. Contact LanDeng for formula, format, packaging and customization."
>
  <article class="article">
    <h1>OEM &amp; Private Label</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      LanDeng offers OEM and private-label development for Chinese botanical incense. We work from
      your target formula, aroma profile, format, and packaging requirements.
    </p>

    <h2>What we can develop with you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Product and formula development (合香 / blended incense)</li>
      <li>Material and ingredient sourcing</li>
      <li>Aroma / fragrance profile direction</li>
      <li>Product formats (stick, cone, sachet, raw material)</li>
      <li>Packaging and private-label presentation</li>
      <li>Sampling, production, quality control, and documentation</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      New to custom manufacturing? Start with our
      <a href="/blog/incense-for-business/">business buying guide</a> and the
      <a href="/blog/oem-private-label-guide/">OEM &amp; private-label guide</a> before you brief a project.
    </p>

    <h2>How to start</h2>
    <p>
      Tell us your target: aroma profile, format, quantity, packaging, and any specification. We
      confirm <strong>sampling, production lead time, MOQ, and documentation</strong> per project —
      we do not publish fixed figures because every project differs.
    </p>

    <h2>What a buyer should verify</h2>
    <p>
      OEM work succeeds or fails on evidence, not on promises. Before committing to a production
      run, a buyer should walk four checks:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Sample first, against your brief.</strong> A sample verifies the product — aroma,
        format, material, and burn — not the supplier's delivery. Evaluate it against the written
        brief, not a general impression. See the
        <a href="/blog/samples-program/">samples program</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Agree the specification sheet.</strong> The spec is what every later batch is
        checked against — ingredients, format, dimensions, burn time, aroma profile, packaging.
        Without a written spec, "matches the sample" is not testable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Request documentation per order, and verify it.</strong> Certificates and test
        reports are claims to verify, not badges to assume: ask for per-order documents and check
        them against the issuing body where the material matters (species, grade, adulteration on
        high-value botanicals). See the
        <a href="/blog/factory-vetting-guide/">factory vetting guide</a> and our
        <a href="/blog/incense-manufacturing-quality-control/">QC overview</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ask how QC works, not just which certificates exist.</strong> Incoming (species,
        grade, adulteration), in-process, and final inspection are the process a manufacturer can
        describe in concrete terms. A wall of certificates without a procedure is not a process.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      What a supplier claim alone cannot establish: capacity figures, per-order compliance,
      lead-time reliability, and batch-to-batch consistency — these are confirmed by documents,
      references, and the first order itself. Start with the
      <a href="/blog/oem-vs-private-label/">OEM vs private label</a> explainer if you are still
      choosing a path.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
